use @piton/belay
from ../Factory import Factory
from ./lib/FactorySkillBase import FactorySkillBase

export skill Factory0010FocusOn extends FactorySkillBase:
    description: Focus factory effort on an engineer-selected objective, order, bottleneck, or capability.
    useWhen: directing or reprioritizing factory work toward a specific goal or problem
    prompt:
        Establish the requested focus from the engineer's instruction and the
        current project context. Read @{Factory.orderPriorities},
        @{Factory.conflictingOrders}, and @{Factory.revisionAuthority}.
        Identify relevant active orders, prerequisites, existing results, and
        constraints. Ask only if the intended focus cannot be inferred.

        Reuse existing work and adjust priorities through factory operations.
        Submit or revise an order when execution is requested; a request to
        investigate or plan remains within that scope. Use a mid-tier workflow
        proposer by default and escalate difficult planning. Delegate focused
        briefs containing objective, requirements, expected output, completion
        criteria, and links to relevant context. Higher-level agents assess
        outcomes without taking over substantial implementation themselves.

        Focus does not silently cancel unrelated work or expand budgets.
        Favor the highest-priority order while other work uses capacity it
        cannot use. For conflicts with earlier intent, assess what can be
        reused or replaced and obtain engineer direction unless replacement
        is already explicit. Apply authorized revisions and supersession rules.
        Resolve conflicting plan revisions through another authorized agent.

        If the focus is an integration problem, create reusable wiring around
        known deterministic operations and require its standard check before
        use. If it calls for a new factory capability, prefer adequate existing
        capabilities first and follow enhancement approval. If it calls for
        review or integration, use risk-appropriate independent assessment and
        project-defined checks on combined changes. Keep routine scheduling,
        execution, and resource enforcement in code.
    result:
        Report the selected focus, affected orders and priorities, delegated
        work, preserved independent work, and decisions awaiting input.
